A variable has a declared type. Use `double` for a numeric input, `boolean` for a decision, and `int` for a count; these are primitive values. A `DcMotor` variable instead holds a reference to a hardware object. A declaration introduces a variable; assignment changes its value. `=` assigns, while `==` compares primitive values. A local variable must receive a value before you read it. Braces group statements and semicolons finish declarations and expression statements.

A class groups behavior. A method receives typed parameters and may return a value; its local variables belong to that invocation. The `if` branch below selects a zero request when input is disabled. Otherwise unary minus reverses the supplied axis sign. The numbers here are mathematical values, not measured motor performance. `static` lets this calculation run without constructing an object. A boolean condition also controls a `while` loop; the condition is checked before each iteration. Repeatedly reading the input differs from storing one initial reading.

This standalone calculation does not send a hardware command. Explain its result for negative, zero and positive inputs with both enabled states before using it in robot code. Java 8 syntax is the target of these examples; the JDK used to build the Android project is a separate setting.

```java
public final class InputMath {
    public static double requestedPower(double axis, boolean enabled) {
        if (!enabled) return 0.0;
        return -axis;
    }
}
```

An OpMode is the SDK entry point selected in Driver Station. For `LinearOpMode`, implement `runOpMode()` and perform setup before `waitForStart()`. Waiting for START is not permission to assume START occurred: check `isStopRequested()` after the wait. Keep repeated work inside `while (opModeIsActive())` so the loop can end when STOP is requested. In SDK 11.1.0 this active check includes the started/stop state and yields through the SDK idle mechanism. Avoid unbounded waits that never check stopping.

The iterative `OpMode` alternative has `init()` once after INIT, repeated `init_loop()` before START, `start()` once at START, repeated `loop()` while running, and `stop()` once at STOP. Do not put your own infinite loop inside an iterative callback; return so the SDK can schedule the next callback. Choose one lifecycle style for a given OpMode.

The complete example only counts software observations and sends telemetry. It makes no actuator command, and the count is not a time measurement. `@TeleOp` registers the class with a menu name; `@Override` marks the implemented SDK method. Registration and a successful build do not demonstrate deployment or robot behavior. On a real project, initialization/start/stop observations require a deliberate supervised run.

```java
package org.firstinspires.ftc.teamcode;

import com.qualcomm.robotcore.eventloop.opmode.LinearOpMode;
import com.qualcomm.robotcore.eventloop.opmode.TeleOp;

@TeleOp(name = "FoundationLifecycle", group = "Learning")
public class FoundationLifecycle extends LinearOpMode {
    @Override
    public void runOpMode() {
        telemetry.addData("phase", "initialized");
        telemetry.update();
        waitForStart();
        if (isStopRequested()) return;
        int observations = 0;
        while (opModeIsActive()) {
            observations++;
            telemetry.addData("observations", observations);
            telemetry.update();
        }
    }
}
```

Hardware mapping connects a configured name and expected Java device type to an object. In initialization, `hardwareMap.get(DcMotor.class, "REPLACE_WITH_DS_MOTOR_NAME")` asks for a motor with that configured name. Replace the placeholder with your actual Driver Station configuration name; it is not a suggested physical device name. Confirm both the configured type and the name. A missing name or incompatible type can cause lookup to fail; renaming a Java variable does not change Driver Station configuration. Mapping may initialize a device, so keep it in the initialization phase rather than repeatedly looking it up during the active loop.

`gamepad1.left_stick_y` is the current left stick vertical axis field; `gamepad1.a` is a boolean button field. Read input in each active iteration when you need the current value. The sample sends the unmodified axis for observation; a sign convention is not a verified motor direction. `getCurrentPosition()` reports encoder counts, not a distance in physical units. Do not assume an encoder zero, gear ratio, wheel size or travel distance without your own configuration and evidence.

`telemetry.addData(...)` adds a labelled value; `telemetry.update()` requests transmission. Call update explicitly in a linear OpMode; the iterative SDK sends updates after its loop callbacks. Updates may be rate limited, so the screen does not prove every iteration arrived. A shown zero is a value; missing or stale data is a different observation. This example never sets motor power. It requires a real correctly configured motor to initialize and is not a hardware-free executable demo.

```java
package org.firstinspires.ftc.teamcode;

import com.qualcomm.robotcore.eventloop.opmode.LinearOpMode;
import com.qualcomm.robotcore.eventloop.opmode.TeleOp;
import com.qualcomm.robotcore.hardware.DcMotor;

@TeleOp(name = "FoundationObservation", group = "Learning")
public class FoundationObservation extends LinearOpMode {
    @Override
    public void runOpMode() {
        DcMotor observedMotor = hardwareMap.get(
            DcMotor.class, "REPLACE_WITH_DS_MOTOR_NAME");
        telemetry.addData("phase", "mapped");
        telemetry.update();
        waitForStart();
        if (isStopRequested()) return;
        while (opModeIsActive()) {
            telemetry.addData("stickY", gamepad1.left_stick_y);
            telemetry.addData("buttonA", gamepad1.a);
            telemetry.addData("encoder", observedMotor.getCurrentPosition());
            telemetry.update();
        }
    }
}
```

Place project code under `TeamCode/src/main/java/org/firstinspires/ftc/teamcode` with the matching package declaration. A public top-level class belongs in a file with that class name. Imports make SDK type names available; they do not create objects or configure devices. Keep names descriptive and distinguish a calculated request from a measured result.

Keep the OpMode responsible for lifecycle order and reading current input. A small separate class is useful when it gives a reusable calculation a clear meaning. The standalone file below is `FoundationInput.java`; an OpMode in the same package can call `FoundationInput.requestedPower(gamepad1.left_stick_y, gamepad1.a)` in an active loop and display the returned value. The helper has no SDK or hardware dependency and never sends power. Do not move hardware lookup into it merely to shorten the OpMode. Do not introduce extra classes for every statement.

Use a comment to explain a non-obvious assumption, such as how an axis sign is interpreted. Review code by following input, calculation and observation in that order, then identify lifecycle boundaries. Preserve team code and existing package organization when adapting the example; these paths show the standard pinned sample layout and are not permission to replace an existing project.

```java
package org.firstinspires.ftc.teamcode;

public final class FoundationInput {
    public static double requestedPower(double axis, boolean enabled) {
        if (!enabled) return 0.0;
        return -axis;
    }
}
```
